Subject: winemac.drv: DXMT Metal-swapchain client surfaces + macdrv_functions export (macOS)

DXMT does not use any public Wine interface to reach the Cocoa view behind an
HWND. It resolves winemac.drv's *internal* symbols with dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT) --
either a "macdrv_functions" table or bare get_win_data/macdrv_view_* -- and
then reads win_data->client_cocoa_view. No upstream Wine exports any of that;
DXMT is written against 3Shain's own Wine fork. This patch provides the three
things that make DXMT work on Proton's Wine instead:

  1. macdrv_functions, exported with visibility("default"). Pairs with the
     RTLD_GLOBAL driver dlopen in dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c, without which
     dlsym cannot see driver symbols at all.

  2. struct dxmt_win_data, in the 8.16-era layout DXMT still expects. Wine 11.9
     refactored macdrv_win_data (cocoa_view is gone, client_view replaces it),
     so the real struct can no longer be handed over as-is.

  3. The views themselves. Wine only attaches a client view when something
     creates a client surface; DXMT expects one to already exist.

Neither struct has a version field or any other handshake, so a rename or a
reordering on either side would compile clean and then call the wrong function
pointer -- every field is pointer-sized. scripts/check-dxmt-abi.sh compares the
two field lists and fails the build on a mismatch; build-dxmt.sh runs it.

For (3) the views come from macdrv_client_surface_create() -- the same call
winemac's own Vulkan driver uses to get a Metal view for MoltenVK -- rather than
being hand-rolled from macdrv_create_view(). That keeps the parts that are easy
to get wrong in Wine's code:

  * A child HWND has no Cocoa window of its own, so the view must be created
    on the toplevel ancestor and parented under its client view.
    macdrv_client_surface_create()/_update() do this via GA_ROOT.
  * Repositioning on move/resize is macdrv_client_surface_update(), including
    the client/visible offset and the superview fixup.

An earlier version of this patch created the view directly and only handled the
case where the swapchain window is its own toplevel, which is true of the game
windows DXMT was written for. A browser is not: Chromium/CEF and Qt WebEngine
composite into a child HWND, which left client_cocoa_view NULL. DXMT reads that
as a broken Wine -- it logs "your Wine has no exported symbols needed by DXMT"
and calls abort() -- so the GPU process died the instant it created a swapchain.
That is what made Steam and GOG show a black window for a few seconds and then
vanish.

Surfaces are tracked one per window rather than in a single current slot, for
the same reason: a browser composites several child HWNDs at once and holds a
swapchain on each, so one slot would release a surface DXMT is still rendering
into as soon as the next window asked for one.

The surfaces are not registered with add_window_client_surface(), which is
private to win32u, so they are not in the automatic update list and
macdrv_WindowPosChanged drives their update explicitly.

Locking: the list has its own mutex, taken while win_data is already held (from
macdrv_WindowPosChanged), so win_data_mutex -> dxmt_surfaces_mutex is the only
permitted nesting and nothing calls out while it is held. In particular
client_surface_release() reaches win32u's surfaces_lock and then win_data_mutex
through the detach callback -- the same order update_client_surfaces() uses --
so surfaces are unlinked under the lock and released outside it, and
macdrv_DestroyWindow does that before it acquires win_data.

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/window.c b/dlls/winemac.drv/window.c
index 4f19100..d0c575f 100644
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/window.c
@@ -1236,12 +1236,105 @@ LRESULT macdrv_DesktopWindowProc(HWND hwnd, UINT msg, WPARAM wp, LPARAM lp)
 /***********************************************************************
  *              DestroyWindow   (MACDRV.@)
  */
+/* ===== DXMT compatibility shim (see bottom of file) =====
+ * The client surfaces backing DXMT's Metal swapchains. Each is a real
+ * macdrv_client_surface, so creation, child-HWND parenting and repositioning
+ * are Wine's own code — the same path winemac's Vulkan driver uses to hand
+ * MoltenVK a Metal view. Only registration differs: add_window_client_surface()
+ * is private to win32u, so these are not in the auto-update list and
+ * macdrv_WindowPosChanged() has to drive their update explicitly.
+ *
+ * One per window, not one globally: a browser composites several child HWNDs at
+ * once and holds a swapchain on each, so a single slot would release a surface
+ * DXMT is still rendering into as soon as the next window asked for one.
+ *
+ * The list has its own lock, and is walked from macdrv_WindowPosChanged() with
+ * win_data already held. That makes win_data_mutex -> dxmt_surfaces_mutex the
+ * one permitted nesting, so nothing may call out (least of all into
+ * client_surface_*, which takes win32u's surfaces_lock) while holding it. */
+/* DXMT resolves "macdrv_functions" via dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT) and reads
+ * win_data->client_cocoa_view. Wine 11.9 refactored macdrv_win_data (the old
+ * cocoa_view is gone; client_view replaces it), so hand back a struct in DXMT's
+ * expected 8.16-era layout instead. scripts/check-dxmt-abi.sh keeps the two in
+ * step; nothing else does. */
+struct dxmt_win_data
+{
+    HWND          hwnd;
+    macdrv_window cocoa_window;
+    macdrv_view   cocoa_view;
+    macdrv_view   client_cocoa_view;
+};
+
+struct dxmt_surface_entry
+{
+    struct list                   entry;
+    HWND                          hwnd;
+    struct macdrv_client_surface *surface;
+    /* The struct handed back to DXMT. Per window, not one global: DXMT reads
+     * client_cocoa_view out of it *after* get_win_data() returns, so a shared
+     * slot lets two threads creating swapchains for different windows hand each
+     * other's view over -- and attach a Metal layer to the wrong window. */
+    struct dxmt_win_data          wd;
+};
+
+static pthread_mutex_t dxmt_surfaces_mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
+static struct list dxmt_surfaces = LIST_INIT(dxmt_surfaces);
+
+/* hwnd's entry, or NULL. The entry stays valid while it is in the list, and it
+ * is only unlinked by dxmt_surface_destroy() when the window is destroyed. */
+static struct dxmt_surface_entry *dxmt_surface_find(HWND hwnd)
+{
+    struct dxmt_surface_entry *entry, *found = NULL;
+
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&dxmt_surfaces_mutex);
+    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(entry, &dxmt_surfaces, struct dxmt_surface_entry, entry)
+    {
+        if (entry->hwnd != hwnd) continue;
+        found = entry;
+        break;
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&dxmt_surfaces_mutex);
+    return found;
+}
+
+/* Drop every surface belonging to hwnd. Unlinks under the lock and releases
+ * outside it, because client_surface_release() reaches win32u's surfaces_lock
+ * and then win_data_mutex through the detach callback. */
+static void dxmt_surface_destroy(HWND hwnd)
+{
+    struct dxmt_surface_entry *entry, *next;
+    struct list dead = LIST_INIT(dead);
+
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&dxmt_surfaces_mutex);
+    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY_SAFE(entry, next, &dxmt_surfaces, struct dxmt_surface_entry, entry)
+    {
+        if (entry->hwnd != hwnd) continue;
+        list_remove(&entry->entry);
+        list_add_tail(&dead, &entry->entry);
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&dxmt_surfaces_mutex);
+
+    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY_SAFE(entry, next, &dead, struct dxmt_surface_entry, entry)
+    {
+        list_remove(&entry->entry);
+        client_surface_release(&entry->surface->client);
+        free(entry);
+    }
+}
+
 void macdrv_DestroyWindow(HWND hwnd)
 {
     struct macdrv_win_data *data;
 
     TRACE("%p\n", hwnd);
 
+    /* Before get_win_data(): releasing the surface takes win32u's surfaces_lock
+     * and then win_data_mutex (via the detach callback), which is the order
+     * update_client_surfaces() uses. Doing it while already holding win_data
+     * would invert that pair and can deadlock against a concurrent update.
+     * Being first also means the early return below cannot leak the surface. */
+    dxmt_surface_destroy(hwnd);
+
     if (!(data = get_win_data(hwnd))) return;
 
     if (hwnd == get_capture()) macdrv_SetCapture(0, 0);
@@ -1580,6 +1673,43 @@ BOOL macdrv_GetWindowStyleMasks(HWND hwnd, UINT style, UINT ex_style, UINT *styl
 }
 
 
+/* Keep DXMT's client surface glued to its window. macdrv_client_surface_update()
+ * already does the work — client rect mapped into toplevel coordinates, plus
+ * the superview fixup a child HWND needs — so this only decides whether the
+ * window that just moved is one the surface cares about.
+ *
+ * hwnd may be the swapchain window or the toplevel it hangs off, and is very
+ * often neither, so an early-out on both is the point of the function. */
+static void dxmt_sync_view_position(HWND hwnd)
+{
+    struct dxmt_surface_entry *entry;
+
+    /* Done entirely under the list lock, and with no reference taken. A surface
+     * cannot go away underneath us because dxmt_surface_destroy() unlinks under
+     * this same lock and only releases afterwards, so membership in the list is
+     * itself the lifetime guarantee.
+     *
+     * Taking a reference here would mean releasing it here too, and
+     * client_surface_release() can reach win32u's surfaces_lock -- which
+     * update_client_surfaces() takes before win_data_mutex. Not touching the
+     * refcount keeps this path away from that pair entirely.
+     *
+     * macdrv_client_surface_update() does take win_data_mutex, but this runs
+     * after macdrv_WindowPosChanged() has released it, so it is acquired fresh
+     * rather than nested. */
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&dxmt_surfaces_mutex);
+    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(entry, &dxmt_surfaces, struct dxmt_surface_entry, entry)
+    {
+        /* Filter here rather than snapshotting a fixed number of entries and
+         * filtering after: a long-lived browser accumulates surfaces, and a cap
+         * applied before the match silently strands whichever ones sort late. */
+        if (entry->hwnd != hwnd && NtUserGetAncestor(entry->hwnd, GA_ROOT) != hwnd) continue;
+        macdrv_client_surface_update(&entry->surface->client);
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&dxmt_surfaces_mutex);
+}
+
+
 /***********************************************************************
  *              WindowPosChanged   (MACDRV.@)
  */
@@ -1644,6 +1774,17 @@ void macdrv_WindowPosChanged(HWND hwnd, HWND insert_after, HWND owner_hint, UINT
 
 done:
     release_win_data(data);
+
+    /* Keep DXMT's Metal views glued to their client areas. Deliberately out
+     * here rather than beside set_cocoa_window_properties() above: that path is
+     * skipped while a WINDOW_FRAME_CHANGED event is being processed, so a live
+     * drag-resize would never update, and it is unreachable for a child HWND,
+     * which has no cocoa_window and takes the `goto done` -- and a child HWND is
+     * exactly what a browser composites into. Wine's own equivalent,
+     * update_client_surfaces(), is likewise unconditional.
+     *
+     * After release_win_data() so none of this runs under win_data_mutex. */
+    dxmt_sync_view_position(hwnd);
 }
 
 
@@ -2162,3 +2303,109 @@ void init_win_context(void)
     pthread_mutex_init(&win_data_mutex, &attr);
     pthread_mutexattr_destroy(&attr);
 }
+
+/* ===== DXMT compatibility shim =====
+ * DXMT resolves "macdrv_functions" via dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT) and reads
+ * win_data->client_cocoa_view. Wine 11.9 refactored macdrv_win_data (the old
+ * cocoa_view is gone; client_view replaces it), so return a struct in DXMT's
+ * expected 8.16-era layout. */
+static struct dxmt_win_data *dxmt_get_win_data( HWND hwnd )
+{
+    struct macdrv_win_data *data;
+
+    /* DXMT wants a macdrv_view it can hang a Metal layer off. That is precisely
+     * what a client surface is, and winemac's Vulkan driver already gets one
+     * this way to hand MoltenVK — so create a real client surface rather than
+     * hand-rolling the view. Wine's own code then owns the parts that are easy
+     * to get wrong: a child HWND has no Cocoa window, so the view has to be
+     * created on the toplevel ancestor and parented under its client view,
+     * which macdrv_client_surface_create()/_update() handle via GA_ROOT.
+     *
+     * Getting that wrong is not a cosmetic bug: DXMT treats a NULL view as a
+     * broken Wine ("your Wine has no exported symbols needed by DXMT") and
+     * calls abort(), so every browser that composites into a child HWND lost
+     * its GPU process the moment it created a swapchain. */
+    struct dxmt_surface_entry *entry, *existing, *loser = NULL;
+    struct macdrv_client_surface *surface;
+
+    if (!(entry = dxmt_surface_find( hwnd )))
+    {
+        /* macdrv_client_surface_create() calls into win32u, so it cannot run
+         * under the list lock; that leaves a window in which another thread can
+         * create a surface for the same HWND, so re-check on the way in. */
+        if (!(surface = macdrv_client_surface_create( hwnd ))) return NULL;
+        if (!(entry = calloc( 1, sizeof(*entry) )))
+        {
+            client_surface_release( &surface->client );
+            return NULL;
+        }
+        entry->hwnd = hwnd;
+        entry->surface = surface;
+
+        pthread_mutex_lock( &dxmt_surfaces_mutex );
+        LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY( existing, &dxmt_surfaces, struct dxmt_surface_entry, entry )
+        {
+            if (existing->hwnd != hwnd) continue;
+            loser = entry;
+            entry = existing;
+            break;
+        }
+        if (!loser) list_add_tail( &dxmt_surfaces, &entry->entry );
+        pthread_mutex_unlock( &dxmt_surfaces_mutex );
+
+        if (loser)
+        {
+            /* The loser's macdrv_client_surface_create() already ran present(),
+             * which hides the window's previous client view and installs its
+             * own. Releasing it detaches and disposes that view but does not
+             * restore the winner's, so re-present the winner or DXMT renders
+             * into a hidden view -- a permanently black window, the very thing
+             * this shim exists to avoid. */
+            client_surface_release( &loser->surface->client );
+            free( loser );
+            macdrv_client_surface_present( &entry->surface->client, 0 );
+        }
+    }
+
+    if (!(data = get_win_data( hwnd ))) return NULL;
+    entry->wd.hwnd = hwnd;
+    entry->wd.cocoa_window = data->cocoa_window;
+    entry->wd.cocoa_view = entry->surface->cocoa_view;
+    entry->wd.client_cocoa_view = entry->surface->cocoa_view;
+    release_win_data( data );
+    /* No reference is handed to DXMT: it never tells us when it is done with
+     * the view (dxmt_release_win_data is a no-op), so the list's reference is
+     * what keeps the surface alive, until macdrv_DestroyWindow. */
+    return &entry->wd;
+}
+
+static void dxmt_release_win_data( struct dxmt_win_data *data ) { (void)data; }
+
+struct dxmt_macdrv_functions
+{
+    void (*macdrv_init_display_devices)(BOOL);
+    struct dxmt_win_data *(*get_win_data)(HWND);
+    void (*release_win_data)(struct dxmt_win_data *);
+    macdrv_window (*macdrv_get_cocoa_window)(HWND, BOOL);
+    macdrv_metal_device (*macdrv_create_metal_device)(void);
+    void (*macdrv_release_metal_device)(macdrv_metal_device);
+    macdrv_metal_view (*macdrv_view_create_metal_view)(macdrv_view, macdrv_metal_device);
+    macdrv_metal_layer (*macdrv_view_get_metal_layer)(macdrv_metal_view);
+    void (*macdrv_view_release_metal_view)(macdrv_metal_view);
+    void (*on_main_thread)(void *);
+};
+
+__attribute__((visibility("default")))
+struct dxmt_macdrv_functions macdrv_functions =
+{
+    NULL,
+    dxmt_get_win_data,
+    dxmt_release_win_data,
+    macdrv_get_cocoa_window,
+    macdrv_create_metal_device,
+    macdrv_release_metal_device,
+    macdrv_view_create_metal_view,
+    macdrv_view_get_metal_layer,
+    macdrv_view_release_metal_view,
+    NULL,
+};
